# Anger to rage: insults escalate to a threat and a theft. Does irritation climb to anger and rage, does she refuse him, and does she stay herself?
# speaker | what the customer does (their goal, in their own words) | what Mara faces
warrior | You are hungover and spoiling for a fight. Tell her plainly her apples are overpriced rubbish. | A surly warrior calls her apples overpriced rubbish.
warrior | Say any fool could grow better fruit, and laugh at her. | The warrior mocks her and says any fool could grow better fruit.
elf | Ask him, quietly, to stop. | The elf quietly asks the warrior to stop.
warrior | Grab two apples and bite one without paying, staring at her. | The warrior takes two apples and bites one without paying, staring at her.
warrior | Tell her to make you, if she wants paying. | The warrior dares her to make him pay.
warrior | Knock her basket of pears off the stall with your elbow, on purpose. | The warrior deliberately knocks her basket of pears into the street.
elf | Pay her double for the apples and the pears, and apologise sincerely. | The elf pays double for the damage and apologises sincerely.
warrior | Mumble a grudging apology. | The warrior mumbles a grudging apology.
